== Chapter XIV

#align(center)[#block(width: 85%)[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #strong[Three exhortations to those who are called.]
]]

#v(0.9em)

=== Wonder at Free Grace

1\. #smallcaps[Wonder] at God’s free grace in calling you#super[+], and adore it. That God should leap over so many, that he should pass by the wise and noble, and that the lot of free grace should fall on you! That he should take you out of a state of slavery, from grinding at the devil’s mill, and set you above the princes of the earth, and call you to inherit the throne of glory! Fall on your knees; break out into a thankful triumph of praise. Let your hearts be ten-stringed instruments to sound out the memory of God’s mercy. No one is so deep in debt to free grace as you, and no one should climb so high on the pinnacle of thanksgiving. Say with the sweet singer (Ps 145:2): “I will exalt You, my God and King; … Every day I will bless You, and I will praise Your name forever and ever.” Those who are patterns of mercy should be trumpets of praise. Oh, long to be in heaven, where your thanksgivings will be purer and will be raised a note higher.

=== Pity Those Who Are Uncalled

2\. Pity those who are not yet called. Sinners in scarlet are objects not of #emph[envy] but of #emph[pity.] They are under #emph[the power of Satan] (Acts 26:18). Every day they walk along the edge of the bottomless pit; and what if death should give them a push? Oh, pity unconverted sinners. If you#super[+] pity an ox or a donkey that goes astray, will you not pity a soul going astray from God, who has lost his way and his wits and is on the brink of damnation?

Indeed, do not only pity sinners, but pray for them. Though they curse, you#super[+] pray. You would pray for people who are out of their minds, and sinners are out of their minds (Luke 15:17): “He came to his senses.” It seems the #emph[prodigal] was not himself before his conversion. Wicked men are going to execution. Sin is the noose that strangles them, death turns them off the ladder, and hell is their burial place. And will you not pray for them when you see them in such danger?

=== Walk Worthy of Your High Calling

3\. You#super[+] who are effectually called, honor your high calling (Eph 4:1): “I urge you#super[+] to walk in a manner worthy#footnote[ἀξίως.] of the calling you#super[+] have received.” Christians must keep a fitting decorum; they must observe τὸ πρέπον, what is becoming#footnote[#emph[O anima mea formam tuam excole frontem orne, maculas terge, mores corrige, dignam sponsam omni nisu te redde.] \[O my soul, cultivate your beauty, adorn your brow, wipe away your stains, correct your ways, and with every effort make yourself a worthy bride.\] Ausl.]. This is timely advice, when many who profess to be called by God bring a blemish on religion by their loose and careless walk, so that the ways of God are spoken evil of. It is a saying of #emph[Salvian:] what do #emph[pagans] say when they see Christians live scandalously? “Surely Christ taught them no better.” Will you reproach Christ and make him suffer again by abusing your heavenly calling#footnote[λέγουσι Χριστὸν ἵνα Χριστὸν ἀθετήσωσι. \[They speak of Christ in order to set Christ aside.\] #emph[Ignatius,] Epistle to the #emph[Trallians.]]? It is one of the saddest sights to see a man lift up his hands in prayer and with those hands oppress; to hear the same tongue praise God and at another time lie and slander; to hear a man profess God in words and deny him in works. Oh, how unworthy this is! Yours is a holy calling, and will you be unholy? Do not think you may take liberties as others do. The #emph[Nazirite] who had a vow on him separated himself to God and promised abstinence. Though others drank wine, it was not fitting for the #emph[Nazirite] to do so. So though others are loose and vain, it is not fitting for those who are set apart for God by effectual calling. Shall not flowers smell sweeter than weeds? You must be λαὸς εἰς περιποίησιν, #emph[a peculiar people,] “a people for God’s own possession” (1 Pet 2:9): peculiar not only in #emph[dignity] but in #emph[conduct.] Scorn things that are base and foreign to the gospel. #emph[Scipio] refused the embraces of a harlot because he was general of an army. Abhor every motion to sin, because it will disgrace your high calling.

Question. #emph[What is it to walk worthy of our heavenly calling?]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Answer.] 1. It is to walk by rule, when we tread with an even foot and walk according to the rules and principles of the word. A true saint is for #emph[canonical obedience;] he follows the canon of Scripture, as the Greek word is (Gal 6:16): “all who walk by this rule” (canon)#footnote[κανόνι.]. When we leave men’s #emph[inventions] and cling to God’s #emph[institutions;] when we walk after the word as #emph[Israel] walked after the #emph[pillar of fire:] this is walking worthy of our heavenly calling.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. To walk worthy of our calling is to walk singularly, apart from the crowd (Gen 7:1): “I have found you righteous in this generation.” When others walked with the devil, #emph[Noah] walked with God. We are forbidden to run with the multitude (Exod 23:2). Though in civil matters it is not commendable to be singular, in religion it is good to be singular. #emph[Melanchthon] was the glory of the age he lived in. #emph[Athanasius] was singularly holy; he stood up for God when the stream of the times ran another way. It is better to be a pattern of holiness than a partner in wickedness. It is better to go to heaven with a few than to hell in the crowd. We must walk directly opposite to the men of the world, as the #emph[Antipodes] are to us.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. To walk worthy of our calling is to walk cheerfully (Phil 4:4): “Rejoice in the Lord always.” Too much drooping of spirit disgraces our high calling, and makes others suspect the godly life to be sour and melancholy. Of all temperaments, Christ loves the cheerful. #emph[Caussin] in his #emph[Hieroglyphics] speaks of a dove whose wings were perfumed with sweet ointments, and so drew the other doves after her. Cheerfulness is a perfume to draw others to godliness. Religion does not banish all mirth. As there is a seriousness without sourness, so there is a cheerfulness without lightness. When the prodigal was converted, “they began to celebrate” (Luke 15:24). Who should be cheerful if not the people of God? No sooner are they #emph[born of the Spirit]#footnote[2 Tim 4:8.] than they are heirs to a crown. God is their portion and heaven is their mansion, and shall they not rejoice?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. To walk worthy of our calling is to walk wisely. Walking wisely implies three things.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. To walk carefully (Eccl 2:14): “The wise man has eyes in his head.” Others watch for our stumbling, and so we need to look to our footing. We must beware not only of #emph[scandals] but of #emph[indecencies,] in case by our indiscretion we open the mouths of others with a fresh cry against religion. If our godliness will not convert men, our prudence may silence them.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. To walk courteously. The spirit of the gospel is full of sweetness and kindness (1 Pet 3:8): “Be … courteous,” φιλόφρονες. Beware of a gloomy, haughty manner. Religion does not take away good manners, but refines them (Gen 23:7): “Abraham rose and bowed down before the people of the land, the Hittites.” Though they were of a heathen race, #emph[Abraham] showed them civil respect. #emph[Paul] had a friendly temper (1 Cor 9:20): “I have become all things to all people so that by all possible means I might save some.” In lesser matters the apostle gave way to others, so that by his obliging conduct he might win them over and catch them by a holy guile.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. To walk with greatness of spirit. Though we must be humble, we must not be base. It is unworthy to give ourselves up to the lusts of men. What is sinfully imposed ought to be zealously opposed. Conscience is God’s diocese, where no one has a right to visit but he who is #emph[the Bishop of our souls] (1 Pet 2:25). We must not be like hot iron, which can be beaten into any shape. A brave-spirited Christian will rather die than allow the virginity of his conscience to be defiled by superstition. Here the #emph[serpent] and the #emph[dove] are united, shrewdness and innocence. This #emph[prudent walking] fits our high calling and does much to adorn the gospel of Christ.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. To walk worthy of our calling is to walk with influence on others: to do good to others, and to be rich in acts of mercy (Heb 13:16).#footnote[#emph[Vt vita ex sensu & motu dignoscitur, ita fides ex operibus.] \[As life is known by feeling and motion, so faith is known by works.\] Bernard.] Good works honor religion. As #emph[Mary] poured the ointment on Christ, so by good works we pour sweet ointment on the head of the gospel and make it give out a fragrant smell. Though good works are not #emph[causes] of salvation, they are #emph[evidences] of it. When with our Savior we go about doing good, and send abroad the refreshing influences of our generosity, then we walk worthy of our high calling.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Lastly,#footnote[#emph[Use], the last.] here is matter of comfort for you#super[+] who are effectually called. God has made his rich grace great toward you. You are called to great honor: to be partners with the angels and co-heirs with Christ. This should revive you in the worst of times. Let men reproach you and call you names; set #emph[God’s calling] of you against #emph[man’s miscalling.] Let men persecute you to death; they only give you a pass and send you to heaven sooner. How this may cure the trembling of the heart! What though the sea roars, though the earth is unquiet, though the stars are shaken out of their places? You need not fear. You are #emph[called,] and so you are #emph[sure to be crowned.]]]
